{"acceptance_length": 7.2894736842105265, "acceptance_lengths": [1, 5, 3, 5, 3, 3, 10, 6, 2, 2, 16, 13, 16, 16, 16, 16, 15, 3, 5, 7, 16, 15, 8, 1, 1, 5, 5, 1, 2, 3, 11, 13, 16, 8, 3, 1, 1, 4], "accepted_draft_lengths": [0, 4, 2, 4, 2, 2, 9, 5, 1, 1, 15, 12, 15, 15, 15, 15, 14, 2, 4, 6, 15, 14, 7, 0, 0, 4, 4, 0, 1, 2, 10, 12, 15, 7, 2, 0, 0, 3], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 23463924224, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 23463915008, "benchmark": "mbpp", "completion": "To find the **n'th Lucas number**, we can use the recurrence relation:\n\n$$\nL(n) = L(n-1) + L(n-2)\n$$\n\nWith the base cases:\n- $ L(0) = 2 $\n- $ L(1) = 1 $\n\nHere's the Python function that implements this:\n\n```python\ndef find_lucas(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 2\n    elif n == 1:\n        return 1\n    else:\n        a, b = 2, 1\n        for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n            a, b = b, a + b\n        return b\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- We start with the first two Lucas numbers: `a = L(0) = 2`, `b = L(1) = 1`.\n- For each number from 2 to `n`, we update `a` and `b` to move the sequence forward.\n- Finally, `b` will be the `n`th Lucas number.\n\n### Test Cases:\n```python\nassert find_lucas(9) == 76\nassert find_lucas(4) == 7\nassert find_lucas(3) == 4\n```\n\nAll assertions will pass with this implementation.", "decode_seconds": 1.3939053784124553, "draft_calls": 38, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 85, "mapper_checkpoint_sha256": "044134103e0118de088370259081aa2b8a5590279fc18aa4ef60fc10ed64f821", "method": "relay_weight1536", "output_hash": "aa75431bd554e688ab910a6f93b8c76e8271623c9bbe5cf696acd3aa82009dfe", "output_tokens": 276, "peak_allocated_memory_bytes": 23579273216, "peak_reserved_memory_bytes": 23861395456, "problem_id": "Mbpp/389", "profile_regions_ms": {"draft": 240.72800064086914, "prefill_full_target": 32.81740951538086, "prefill_relay": 0.16259199380874634, "relay": 8.461024031043053, "unattributed_runtime": 16.00145423039794, "verification_full_target": 1130.565