{"acceptance_length": 7.462962962962963, "acceptance_lengths": [4, 10, 7, 2, 1, 4, 8, 9, 2, 9, 8, 1, 1, 2, 8, 3, 4, 2, 4, 1, 10, 1, 3, 2, 5, 2, 2, 2, 2, 4, 2, 7, 4, 2, 5, 5, 8, 3, 11, 3, 16, 16, 16, 3, 14, 8, 16, 6, 16, 16, 16, 14, 4, 2, 3, 15, 2, 2, 2, 1, 5, 2, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, 2, 8, 2, 5, 10, 4, 4, 2, 16, 11, 16, 3, 11, 16, 10, 16, 16, 16, 16, 14, 16, 16, 16, 4, 16, 16, 8, 13, 3, 2, 5, 4, 1, 4, 16, 16, 16, 16, 16, 16, 12], "accepted_draft_lengths": [3, 9, 6, 1, 0, 3, 7, 8, 1, 8, 7, 0, 0, 1, 7, 2, 3, 1, 3, 0, 9, 0, 2, 1, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 3, 1, 6, 3, 1, 4, 4, 7, 2, 10, 2, 15, 15, 15, 2, 13, 7, 15, 5, 15, 15, 15, 13, 3, 1, 2, 14, 1, 1, 1, 0, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 7, 1, 4, 9, 3, 3, 1, 15, 10, 15, 2, 10, 15, 9, 15, 15, 15, 15, 13, 15, 15, 15, 3, 15, 15, 7, 12, 2, 1, 4, 3, 0, 3, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 11], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 23463925248, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 23463915520, "benchmark": "mbpp", "completion": "To solve the problem of finding the **maximum difference between the number of 0s and 1s** in any sub-string of a binary string, we can use a **prefix sum** approach.\n\n### Key Idea:\n- Treat `0` as `+1` and `1` as `-1`.\n- Compute a running sum as we traverse the string.\n- The difference between the current sum and the minimum sum seen so far gives the maximum difference between 0s and 1s in a sub-string.\n\n### Why this works:\n- If we have a running sum `sum`, then the difference between the current sum and the minimum sum up to that point gives the maximum number of 0s minus 1s in a sub-string ending at that index.\n\n---\n\n### Python Code:\n\n```python\ndef find_length(s):\n    max_diff = 0\n    current_sum = 0\n    min_sum = 0\n\n    for char in s:\n        if char == '0':\n            current_sum += 1\n        else:\n            current_sum -= 1\n\n        if current_sum - min_sum > max_diff:\n            max_diff = current_sum - min_sum\n\n        if current_sum < min_sum:\n            min_sum = current_sum\n\n    return max_diff\n```\n\n---\n\n### Test Cases:\n\n```python\nassert find_length(\"11000010001\") == 6\nassert find_length(\"10111\") == 1\nassert find_length(\"11011101100101\") == 2\n```\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of the First Test Case:\n\nInput: `\"11000010001\"`\n\nLet's compute the running sum:\n\n| Index | Char | Value | Running Sum | Min Sum | Diff (Current - Min) |\n|-------|------|-------|-------------|---------|----------------------|\n| 0     | '1'  | -1    | -1          | -1      | 0                    |\n| 1     | '1'  | -1    | -2          | -2      | 0                    |\n| 2     | '0'  | +1    | -1          | -2      | 1                    |\n| 3     | '0'  | +1    | 0           | -2      | 2                    |\n| 4     | '0'  | +1    | 1           | -2      | 3                    |\n| 5     | '0'  | +1    | 2           | -2      | 4                    |\n| 6     | '1'  | -1    | 1           | -2      | 3                    |\n| 7     | '0'  | +1    | 2           | -2      | 4                    |\n| 8     | '0'  | +1    | 3           | -2      | 5                    |\n| 9     | '0'  | +1    | 4           | -2      | 6                    |\n| 10    | '1'  | -1    | 3           | -2      | 5                    |\n\nSo the maximum difference is **6**, which matches the expected result.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n```python\ndef find_length(s):\n    max_diff = 0\n    current_sum = 0\n    min_sum = 0\n\n    for char in s:\n        if char == '0':\n            current_sum += 1\n        else:\n            current_sum -= 1\n\n        if current_sum - min_sum > max_diff:\n            max_diff = current_sum - min_sum\n\n        if current_sum < min_sum:\n            min_sum = current_sum\n\n    return max_diff\n```", "decode_seconds": 4.024502163287252, "draft_calls": 108, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 134, "mapper_checkpoint_sha256": "ece1269540becf19b73b75b0ca48a5dfd6178ffe372c6606870efbbab8ae580a", "method": "relay_base", "output_hash": "c66404a5554