{"acceptance_length": 9.03125, "acceptance_lengths": [9, 4, 6, 4, 10, 12, 16, 16, 13, 4, 7, 2, 13, 10, 2, 14, 7, 16, 7, 16, 5, 16, 12, 16, 16, 9, 8, 7, 2, 3, 2, 5], "accepted_draft_lengths": [8, 3, 5, 3, 9, 11, 15, 15, 12, 3, 6, 1, 12, 9, 1, 13, 6, 15, 6, 15, 4, 15, 11, 15, 15, 8, 7, 6, 1, 2, 1, 4], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 23463925760, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 23463915520, "benchmark": "mbpp", "completion": "Here's a Python function that checks whether a list is sorted in **non-decreasing order** (i.e., each element is less than or equal to the next one):\n\n```python\ndef issort_list(lst):\n    for i in range(len(lst) - 1):\n        if lst[i] > lst[i + 1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- The function iterates through the list from the first element to the second-to-last element.\n- It compares each element with the next one.\n- If any element is greater than the next one, the list is not sorted, and the function returns `False`.\n- If the loop completes without finding any such pair, the list is sorted, and the function returns `True`.\n\n### Test Cases:\n```python\nassert issort_list([1,2,4,6,8,10,12,14,16,17]) == True\nassert issort_list([1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 20, 17]) == False\nassert issort_list([1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10,15,14,20]) == False\n```\n\nAll of these assertions will pass with the above function.", "decode_seconds": 1.1752335680648685, "draft_calls": 32, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 179, "mapper_checkpoint_sha256": "a53b21494b39f7139d3a04b206a5698dbf22fbd0fa7192f8f9cc577bcf4a59e9", "method": "relay_two", "output_hash": "3a493b3fe6e1c270ed79fe207bab3a248b2cbae05a5e0de5193a5c354ba5c721", "output_tokens": 288, "peak_allocated_memory_bytes": 23627281408, "peak_reserved_memory_bytes": 23945281536, "problem_id": "Mbpp/567", "profile_regions_ms": {"draft": 203.68115186691284, "prefill_full_target": 35.09968185424805, "prefill_relay": 0.16035200655460358, "relay": 6.1935679614543915, "unattribu